'''Emanuel David''' "'''Manu'''" '''Ginóbili''' ({{IPAc-en|lang|ˈ|m|æ|n|uː|_|dʒ|ɪ|ˈ|n|oʊ|b|l|i}} {{respelling|MAN|oo|_|jin|OH|blee}},<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nba.com/global/intl_player_pronunciation_070426.html|title=International NBA Players - Pronunciation Guide|website=[[NBA.com]]|access-date=10 June 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100108185208/http://www.nba.com/global/intl_player_pronunciation_070426.html|archive-date=8 January 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> {{IPA|es|ˈmanu ʝiˈnoβili|lang}};<ref>{{cite video|url=https://youtube.com/watch?v=kYmYjDVh4m8 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211211/kYmYjDVh4m8| archive-date=2021-12-11 |url-status=live|title=Entrevista a Manu Ginóbili en Unidos por Argentina|language=es|work=YouTube|publisher=Televisión Pública|date=5 April 2020|access-date=10 June 2021}}{{cbignore}}</ref> born 28 July 1977<ref name="bio"/>) is an Argentine former professional [[basketball]] player. During a 23-year professional career, Ginóbili played in Argentina, in Italy, and in the [[National Basketball Association|NBA]]; he also represented Argentina in international competition. A shooting guard, he is best known for playing for the [[San Antonio Spurs]] of the NBA from 2002 to 2018. Ginóbili, [[Tim Duncan]], and [[Tony Parker]] became known as the Spurs' "[[Big Three (San Antonio Spurs)|Big Three]]"; together, the trio won four NBA championships. In April 2022, Ginóbili was announced as a first-ballot inductee into the [[Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 April 2022|title=Manu Ginobili, Lindsay Whalen, Swin Cash, Tim Hardaway among 2022 Basketball Hall of Fame inductees|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/manu-ginobili-lindsay-whalen-swin-cash-among-2022-basketball-hall-of-fame-inductees-175949934.html|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220403224056/https://sports.yahoo.com/manu-ginobili-lindsay-whalen-swin-cash-among-2022-basketball-hall-of-fame-inductees-175949934.html|archive-date=3 April 2022|access-date=3 April 2022}}</ref>

Coming from a family of professional basketball players, Ginóbili spent the early part of his career in [[Argentina]] and [[Italy]], winning several individual and team honors. Ginóbili's stint with Italian club Kinder Bologna was particularly successful, as he won two [[Lega Basket Serie A MVP|Italian League MVP]] awards, the [[EuroLeague Final Four MVP|EuroLeague Finals MVP]], the [[2001 Euroleague Finals|2001 EuroLeague]] championship and the [[European Basketball Triple Crown|Triple Crown]]. After joining the San Antonio Spurs, Ginóbili was named an [[List of NBA All-Stars|All-Star]] in [[2005 NBA All-Star Game|2005]] and [[2011 NBA All-Star Game|2011]] and was selected to the [[All-NBA Third Team]] twice. In the [[2007–08 NBA season|2007–08 season]], he was named the [[NBA Sixth Man of the Year]]. The Spurs made the playoffs in each of Ginóbili's 16 seasons. He is regarded as one of the greatest [[Latin America|Latin American]] players, [[sixth man|sixth men]], and [[draft steal]]s in NBA history.<ref>Multiple sources:
*{{Cite web |last=Bailey |first=Andy |title=Best Latin American Players in NBA History |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2621359-best-latin-american-players-in-nba-history |access-date=2025-08-18 |website=bleacherreport.com |language=en}}
*{{Cite web |last=Bassett |first=Bailey |date=2024-06-17 |title=Biggest steals in NBA Draft history, ranked |url=https://clutchpoints.com/nba/nba-stories/biggest-steals-in-nba-draft-history-ranked |access-date=2025-08-18 |website=ClutchPoints {{!}} NBA News |language=en}}
*{{Cite web |last=Urbina |first=Frank |title=Ranking the best Sixth Men in NBA history |url=https://www.hoopshype.com/story/sports/nba/rankings/2025/04/22/nba-ranking-best-sixth-men-backups-reserves-bench-players-history/83207075007/ |access-date=2025-08-18 |website=HoopsHype |language=en-US}}
*{{Cite web |title=The Best South American NBA Players Of All Time |url=https://www.ranker.com/list/south-american-nba-players/ranker-nba |access-date=2023-05-17 |website=Ranker |language=en |archive-date=17 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230517203055/https://www.ranker.com/list/south-american-nba-players/ranker-nba |url-status=live }}
